1. First, make sure you have added the "docx4j" library to your Java project. You can do this by downloading the library from the docx4j website and adding the JAR file to your classpath.
2. Next, import the necessary classes from the "docx4j" library. You will need to import the "WordprocessingMLPackage" and "Docx4J" classes, as well as any other classes you may need depending on the specific elements you want to include in your JSON output.
3. Load your Word document into a "WordprocessingMLPackage" object. You can do this by using the "WordprocessingMLPackage.load" method and passing in the path to your Word document file.
4. Use the "Docx4J.toJSON" method to convert your Word document to JSON format. This method takes two arguments: the "WordprocessingMLPackage" object containing your Word document, and a "JSONML" object that specifies the options for the JSON output.
5. You can then use the resulting JSON object as desired, such as by writing it to a file or sending it to an API.

Here is some sample code that demonstrates how to use the "docx4j" library to convert a Word document to JSON format:


```
import org.docx4j.Docx4J;
import org.docx4j.convert.out.json.JSONML;
import org.docx4j.openpackaging.exceptions.Docx4JException;
import org.docx4j.openpackaging.packages.WordprocessingMLPackage;

public class WordToJSON {
 public static void main(String[] args) throws Docx4JException {
 // Load the Word document
 WordprocessingMLPackage wordDoc = WordprocessingMLPackage.load(new File("document.docx"));

 // Convert the Word document to JSON
 JSONML json = Docx4J.toJSON(wordDoc);

 // Print the JSON object
 System.out.println(json.toString());
 }
}
```
This code will load the Word document at "document.docx" and convert it to JSON format using the "Docx4J.toJSON" method. The resulting JSON object is then printed to the console.

