Yes, the "docx4j" Java library can include comments from a Microsoft Word document in the JSON output.

To include comments in the JSON output, you will need to specify the "comments" option when calling the "Docx4J.toJSON" method. This option is a boolean value that determines whether comments should be included in the JSON output.

Here is an example of how you can use the "comments" option to include comments in the JSON output:


```
import org.docx4j.Docx4J;
import org.docx4j.convert.out.json.JSONML;
import org.docx4j.openpackaging.exceptions.Docx4JException;
import org.docx4j.openpackaging.packages.WordprocessingMLPackage;

public class WordToJSON {
 public static void main(String[] args) throws Docx4JException {
 // Load the Word document
 WordprocessingMLPackage wordDoc = WordprocessingMLPackage.load(new File("document.docx"));

 // Create a JSONML object with the "comments" option set to true
 JSONML json = new JSONML();
 json.setComments(true);

 // Convert the Word document to JSON
 json = Docx4J.toJSON(wordDoc, json);

 // Print the JSON object
 System.out.println(json.toString());
 }
}
```
This code will load the Word document at "document.docx" and convert it to JSON format using the "Docx4J.toJSON" method. The "comments" option is set to true in the "JSONML" object, so comments from the Word document will be included in the JSON output. The resulting JSON object is then printed to the console.

