We then create a `log_mytable_changes()` function that loops through each column in `mytable` (excluding the ID and timestamp columns), compares the old and new values, and inserts a row into `mytable_changes` if the value has changed.

Finally, we create a trigger on `mytable` that executes the `log_mytable_changes()` function after each `UPDATE` operation.

With this trigger and logging table in place, you can now easily track any changes made to any column in `mytable`. The `mytable_changes` table provides a human-readable log of all changes, including the date and time of the change, the record ID, the field name, and the old and new values.

